  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14163598/assets/73d739c3d2e4c6bc9d296136ef6f17e7fa0bf565aa4c8c4712e54df4e952935f.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-1,3].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-1,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$",
